<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kayapó</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mebêngôkre</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kayapó are Gê-speaking indigenous peoples of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. The Southern Kayapó of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goias-velho">Goiás</a> and Mato Grosso fought Portuguese miners and settlers in the 18th century and were largely destroyed, while the northern Kayapó, or Mebêngôkre, live today in Pará and Mato Grosso along the Xingu river. Their leaders, such as Raoni, became known worldwide for defending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> forest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kayapo.jpg" alt="Kayapó" width="320" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Apib Comunicação · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tu%C3%ADre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Kayapo live over a vast area of the states of Pará and Mato Grosso in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, south of the Amazon River and along the Xingu River and its tributaries, which has given them the nickname the Xingu. They are one of the subgroups of the Mebêngôkre nation, a name meaning people from the water's source. The name Kayapo came from neighbouring peoples in the early 19th century and means those who look like monkeys, probably after a men's ritual with monkey masks. Subgroups include the Xikrin, Gorotire, Mekranoti and Metyktire, and an estimated 8,638 Kayapo lived in 2018, up from 7,096 in 2003. Their villages typically have about a dozen huts, with a central hut where the men meet to discuss community matters.</p><p>In the 18th century, in the north-east of the present state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a>, the Kayapo first met Portuguese-Brazilian bandeirantes, who were seeking gold, precious stones and Indigenous slaves, and as a result the Kayapo left the area and moved west. The Brazilian government contacted the Kayapo in the 1950s and 1960s with the intention of making peace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Kayapo speak a language of the Jê family, in several dialects, and value oratory so highly that they call themselves those who speak beautifully, Kaben mei. A beautiful name is a sign of wealth, and a naming ceremony requires enough food for a celebration. Men and women wear intricate black body painting, which they say mimics the insects from which their ancestors learned the ways of life. Chiefs wear headdresses of yellow feathers representing the rays of the sun, and women shave a V into their hair. They practise shifting cultivation by slash and burn, growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweet-potato">sweet potatoes</a>, yams, taro, manioc and papaya, and use about 250 food plants and 650 medicinal plants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Their land is arguably the largest tropical protected area in the world, covering 11,346,326 hectares. In the village of Gorotire the Kayapo agreed to gold mining in return for a share of the proceeds and demarcation of their territory. Mining brought money and better housing, education and health, but also mercury pollution that decimated fish, and disease. The Kayapo used forceful tactics to expel loggers and miners in some areas.</p><p>In 1987 the government proposed hydroelectric dams on the Xingu, among them the Belo Monte Dam. Under the leadership of Paulinho Paiakan the Kayapo organised the Altamira Gathering, which drew worldwide media attention and put pressure on the World Bank and the Brazilian government. Sting appeared at the demonstration and in 1989 founded the Rainforest Foundation Fund. In 1992 Conservation International Brazil began a partnership with the Kayapo community of A'Ukre to protect a stand of mahogany.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kayapo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Conflicts with indigenous peoples such as the <b>Kayapó</b> and Xavante were frequent, and the government founded aldeias to settle them.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-goias">Captaincy of Goiás</a></li></ul></section>
